-The disposable role smoke was expanded after review to exercise the remaining explicit destructive-grant branches independently. A runtime identity with only table `REFERENCES`, one with only table `TRIGGER`, and one with only sequence `UPDATE` each fail production connection checkout with the generic privilege-ceiling error. `bash -n scripts/dev/postgres-runtime-role-smoke.sh`, `bash scripts/dev/postgres-runtime-role-smoke.sh`, and all four `tests.test_db_role_policy` cases passed. The smoke uses a digest-pinned disposable PostgreSQL instance; no shared database, Redis state, or external SRO record was changed. These controls close the listed branch-test gap; they do not broaden the guard into a complete ACL allowlist.
+The disposable role smoke was expanded after review to exercise table `REFERENCES`, table `TRIGGER` and sequence `UPDATE` grants independently. A further check considered PostgreSQL `SECURITY DEFINER`: a synthetic function owned by the migration role deleted a row from an owner-protected synthetic table when called by a runtime identity with only `EXECUTE`, confirming this can bypass direct table grants. The production role ceiling now rejects any executable `SECURITY DEFINER` routine in `public`. The same runtime identity is rejected at connection checkout with the generic privilege-ceiling error after the guard is enabled. `bash -n scripts/dev/postgres-runtime-role-smoke.sh`, `bash scripts/dev/postgres-runtime-role-smoke.sh`, and all four `tests.test_db_role_policy` cases passed. This used only a digest-pinned disposable PostgreSQL instance; no shared database, Redis state or external SRO record was changed. The checks close these concrete bypass/test gaps; they do not broaden the guard into a complete ACL allowlist.
